  "worked_solution": "First, let's factor the expression as a perfect square trinomial in ${t}^{2}.$ We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{4}+30{t}^{2}+25 & ={(3{t}^{2})}^{2}+2\\cdot (3{t}^{2})\\cdot 5+{5}^{2} \\\\  & =(3{t}^{2}+5)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we need to factor the sum of squares in the parentheses. To do this, we first write it as a difference. Then, we rewrite the second term using ${\\text{i}}^{2}=-1.$ Finally, we factor as a difference of squares.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{t}^{2}+5 & =3{t}^{2}-(-5) \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-(5{\\text{i}}^{2}) \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t)^{2}-(\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this back into our main expression, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{t}^{4}+30{t}^{2}+25 & =(3{t}^{2}+5)^{2} \\\\  & =((\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i}))^{2} \\\\  & =(\\sqrt{3}t+\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}(\\sqrt{3}t-\\sqrt{5}\\text{i})^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
